\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Curl and the Hodge star}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The undergraduate quotation keeps #{\operatorname{curl}}, and the
forms paragraph keeps the Hodge star #{\star} of its source. Hestenes's
geometric algebra (\citek{hestenes2012clifford}) writes duality as a
product with the pseudoscalar, so it joins the other products of the
algebra: with the convention #{A\wedge\star B=\langle A,B\rangle\psI},
the Hodge dual of a #{k}-vector #{A} is #{\star A=\tilde A\psI}, where
#{\tilde A} reverses the order of the vector factors of #{A}. Since
#{\psI^2=-1} in spacetime, this is #{F\psI^{-1}} on the bivector #{F}
and #{-J\psI^{-1}} on the vector #{J}. The geometric paragraph writes
the exterior part #{\nabla\wedge F}. The pseudoscalar #{\psI} stays
distinct from the identity map #{\bbI}.}
